1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,626
Welcome to lesson six: Services, and how Pods find each other.

2
00:00:04,726 --> 00:00:13,328
You'll give your app a stable address, spread traffic across Pods, expose it inside and outside the cluster, and learn to debug connection problems.

3
00:00:14,678 --> 00:00:20,672
Pods are temporary. Every new Pod gets a new IP address, and a rolling update replaces all of them.

4
00:00:20,772 --> 00:00:26,334
So if your frontend called the API at a Pod IP, it would break the next time that Pod restarted.

5
00:00:26,434 --> 00:00:35,772
A Service solves this. It gives you a stable virtual IP, a stable DNS name, and load balancing across all the ready Pods that match its selector.

6
00:00:37,122 --> 00:00:39,116
Here's what happens inside.

7
00:00:39,216 --> 00:00:45,706
A controller finds every ready Pod matching the Service's selector and lists their IPs in an EndpointSlice.

8
00:00:45,806 --> 00:00:53,448
kube-proxy on every node turns that list into forwarding rules, and CoreDNS answers the Service's name with its ClusterIP.

9
00:00:53,548 --> 00:01:02,822
The ClusterIP is virtual. It only exists as kernel rules, so ping usually fails even when the Service works. Always test with curl or wget.

10
00:01:04,172 --> 00:01:10,422
A Service has its own port, which clients connect to, and a target port, where the app actually listens.

11
00:01:10,522 --> 00:01:18,308
NodePort and LoadBalancer Services also open a node port, between thirty thousand and thirty-two thousand seven hundred sixty-seven.

12
00:01:18,408 --> 00:01:25,570
A good habit is to name the container port and refer to it by name. And if a Service has several ports, each one needs a name.

13
00:01:26,920 --> 00:01:30,658
There are several Service types, and each builds on the one before.

14
00:01:30,758 --> 00:01:41,344
ClusterIP, the default, is reachable only inside the cluster. NodePort also opens a port on every node. LoadBalancer asks the cloud for a real load balancer with an external IP.

15
00:01:41,444 --> 00:01:47,390
ExternalName is just a DNS alias, and a headless Service returns the Pod IPs directly.

16
00:01:47,490 --> 00:01:54,652
On a lab cluster, a LoadBalancer stays pending forever, because there's no cloud to create one. That's expected.

17
00:01:56,002 --> 00:02:03,068
Every Service gets a DNS name: the service name, then the namespace, then svc dot cluster dot local.

18
00:02:03,168 --> 00:02:10,122
Short names work because each Pod has search domains configured. A bare name looks only in the Pod's own namespace.

19
00:02:10,222 --> 00:02:17,960
So the most common bug is calling a Service by its short name from a different namespace. Add the namespace: api svc dot payments.

20
00:02:19,310 --> 00:02:23,928
A few extras. Session affinity sends the same client to the same Pod.

21
00:02:24,028 --> 00:02:31,574
External traffic policy Local keeps the client's real IP address, with the trade-off that only nodes running a Pod accept traffic.

22
00:02:31,674 --> 00:02:37,764
The quickest way to create a Service is kubectl expose, which copies the selector from a Deployment.

23
00:02:39,114 --> 00:02:44,660
In the lab, you'll use a tiny web server called whoami, which replies with its own Pod name.

24
00:02:44,760 --> 00:02:51,954
Fetch the Service six times from a client Pod, and the hostnames rotate between the three Pods. That's load balancing.

25
00:02:52,054 --> 00:02:57,248
DNS resolves the name, but ping fails, because the ClusterIP is virtual.

26
00:02:58,598 --> 00:03:01,136
Then you'll break it in two classic ways.

27
00:03:01,236 --> 00:03:08,670
Scale to zero, and the Service still exists and DNS still works, but there are no endpoints, so every request fails.

28
00:03:08,770 --> 00:03:16,748
Then introduce a typo in the selector. The endpoints become empty, and there's no error message anywhere. It just silently stops working.

29
00:03:18,098 --> 00:03:20,780
When a Service doesn't work, check in this order.

30
00:03:20,880 --> 00:03:26,730
Does it exist in the right namespace? Does it have endpoints? Do the labels match? Are the Pods ready?

31
00:03:26,830 --> 00:03:36,456
Is the app listening on the target port, on all interfaces? Does DNS resolve? Does the Pod work directly with port-forward? And is a network policy blocking it?

32
00:03:36,556 --> 00:03:41,766
If the Pod works directly but not through the Service, the problem is the selector or the ports.

33
00:03:43,116 --> 00:03:44,198
Let's recap.

34
00:03:44,298 --> 00:03:49,604
A Service gives a stable IP, a DNS name, and load balancing to ready Pods.

35
00:03:49,704 --> 00:03:54,514
Know the difference between port, target port and node port, and the Service types.

36
00:03:54,614 --> 00:04:01,632
Use the namespace in the name when calling across namespaces, and when endpoints are empty, check the selector and readiness.

37
00:04:01,732 --> 00:04:07,662
That completes level one. Next, lesson seven starts level two with ConfigMaps and Secrets.
